      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2.59 ± 2.09",
      "bbox": [
        487.91162109375,
        260.5769348144531,
        534.2106323242188,
        277.3891296386719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "estimation diverges largely. We thus had to disable this feature by unrolling the gradients. To fairly compare SDEs and OT,",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        315.7383728027344,
        542.6887817382812,
        324.6449279785156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "we have also unrolled gradient propagation through SDEs without using any implicit/adjoint methods.",
      "bbox": [
        55.082000732421875,
        327.693359375,
        462.1536865234375,
        336.59991455078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The results are detailed in Tables 7 to 10. We clearly see that the diffusion resampling outperforms other methods across",
      "bbox": [
        55.13100051879883,
        345.6263732910156,
        541.4395751953125,
        354.5329284667969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "all the three metrics, in particular for the filtering and parameter estimation errors. The superiority of the log-likelihood",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        357.58135986328125,
        541.4411010742188,
        366.4879150390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "function estimation is not significant due to that the loss function’s magnitude does not change much in Θ, see also Figure 2.",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        369.376953125,
        543.1852416992188,
        378.4429016113281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Let us focus on the diffusion resampling in Tables 7 and 9. In Tables 7 we find that the loss function estimation error seems",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        387.4693603515625,
        541.4402465820312,
        396.37591552734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to be invariant in the time T and steps K when using an ODE solver (except for Lord–Rougemont), giving a relatively large",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        399.26495361328125,
        541.4431762695312,
        408.3309020996094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "error 2.72. With a fixed T, it is not clear if increasing K will improve the log-likelihood estimate. This also resonates with",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        411.2209777832031,
        541.4428100585938,
        420.28692626953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Table 9 that increasing K can possibly lead to worst even exploding parameter estimation. This may be caused by numerical",
      "bbox": [
        55.13100051879883,
        423.17596435546875,
        541.4389038085938,
        432.2419128417969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "errors when back-propagating gradients through SDE solvers which can be addressed by, for instance, Kidger et al. (2021).",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        435.2903747558594,
        543.1839599609375,
        444.1969299316406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "On the other hand, with K fixed, increasing T improves the result. Like in the previous experiments, the Jentzen–Kloeden",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        447.0859680175781,
        541.4357299804688,
        456.15191650390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "exponential integrator consistently achieves the best result, albeit marginally, while Lord–Rougemont is the most sensitive",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        459.2003479003906,
        541.4381103515625,
        468.1069030761719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to K.",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        470.9959716796875,
        77.34774780273438,
        480.0619201660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Table 8 shows the performance of the filtering, and this does not compute any gradients. We see that improving T and",
      "bbox": [
        55.13100051879883,
        488.928955078125,
        541.4407348632812,
        497.9949035644531
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "K improves the filtering estimation in general. This empirically verifies the results from Tables 7 and 9 that efficient and",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        500.88397216796875,
        541.4413452148438,
        509.9499206542969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "accurate back-propagation through SDE solvers may be necessary.",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        512.9983520507812,
        320.7439880371094,
        521.9049072265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Figure 2 shows loss function landscapes estimated by the particle filtering (N = 32 particles) with different resampling",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        530.77197265625,
        541.4359741210938,
        539.8379516601562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "schemes. The calibration parameters for Gumbel-Softmax and soft resampling are 0.2 and 0.1, respectively, and for diffusion,",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        542.8863525390625,
        542.6897583007812,
        551.7929077148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "we use Jentzen–Kloden SDE integrator with T = 2 and K = 4, and for OT ε = 0.3. We see in this figure that multinomial",
      "bbox": [
        55.082000732421875,
        554.6829223632812,
        541.4450073242188,
        563.7489013671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "gives the most noisy estimate, hard for searching the optimum. This is also true for soft resampling, and even at a low",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        566.79736328125,
        541.7964477539062,
        575.7039184570312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "parameter 0.1 which already means very uninformative resampling, the loss function is still rather noisy. On the other hand,",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        578.5929565429688,
        542.68212890625,
        587.658935546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the loss function estimates by diffusion, OT, and Gumbel-Softmax look smooth.",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        590.7073974609375,
        374.233154296875,
        599.6139526367188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "J. Prey-predator model",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        617.7778930664062,
        173.96383666992188,
        628.525634765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Recall our prey-predator (or also called Lokta–Volterra) model",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        638.6453857421875,
        305.4912414550781,
        647.5519409179688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "dC(t) = C(t) (α −β R(t)) dt + σ C(t) dW1(t),",
      "bbox": [
        199.92599487304688,
        677.7509765625,
        396.9580078125,
        695.0360717773438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "dR(t) = R(t) (ζ C(t) −γ) dt + σ R(t) dW2(t),",
      "bbox": [
        200.11700439453125,
        692.6950073242188,
        395.0010070800781,
        709.9801025390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Yj ∼Poisson",
      "bbox": [
        214.6580047607422,
        708.7340087890625,
        270.5550231933594,
        727.5140991210938
      ],
